[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C code uses preprocessor macros [38;5;10mOP[0m and [38;5;10mREPEAT[0m that are set at compile time via [38;5;10m-DOP=sub -DREPEAT=6[0m (as determined by the test vectors). The original Rust translation had two bugs:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [1mWrong operation[22m: The translator hardcoded [38;5;10mOP=add[0m (using [38;5;10mop_add[0m, [38;5;10maccum_add[0m, [38;5;10mINIT_add=0[0m, [38;5;10mSTEP_add[0m which does [38;5;10macc += i[0m), but the test suite was compiled with [38;5;10mOP=sub[0m. The fix was to change all references to use [38;5;10mop_sub[0m, [38;5;10maccum_sub[0m, and the subtraction step [38;5;10macc -= i[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
2. [1mWrong repeat count[22m: The translator used [38;5;10mREPEAT=5[0m (the default in the header), but the tests were compiled with [38;5;10mREPEAT=6[0m. This meant the unrolled loop did 5 iterations (i=0..4, acc=10 for add) instead of 6 (i=0..5, acc=-15 for sub). The fix was to change all [38;5;10mRUN_LOOP[0m expansions and the [38;5;10muse_generated()[0m call to use 6 iterations.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The root cause is that the LLM translator used the default macro values from [38;5;10mmdmacros.h[0m ([38;5;10m#define OP add[0m, [38;5;10m#define REPEAT 5[0m) rather than the actual compile-time overrides specified in [38;5;10mCMakeLists.txt[0m ([38;5;10m-DOP=sub -DREPEAT=6[0m). This is a common pitfall when translating C code that relies on build-system-injected macro definitions.[0m[0m
